1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,190
Gemini Decisions API:

2
00:00:02,630 --> 00:00:05,230
you think 20 questions about 1 insurance claim

3
00:00:05,330 --> 00:00:07,070
actually cost you 20 prompts,

4
00:00:07,480 --> 00:00:09,560
but 1 call picks all 20 answers,

5
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:11,277
each with how sure it is.

6
00:00:11,777 --> 00:00:12,496
Here's how.

7
00:00:12,796 --> 00:00:15,020
Before, you asked a chat model one at a time:

8
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:17,070
the claim sent 20 times,

9
00:00:17,170 --> 00:00:18,320
20 essay answers,

10
00:00:18,620 --> 00:00:19,953
no number for how sure.

11
00:00:20,453 --> 00:00:23,070
Now your app sends the claim once with all 20 questions:

12
00:00:23,690 --> 00:00:26,321
yes or no, multiple choice, a score.

13
00:00:26,621 --> 00:00:27,830
Like one answer sheet.

14
00:00:28,330 --> 00:00:30,934
It doesn't write; it scores the options you gave it.

15
00:00:31,234 --> 00:00:33,910
Each answer gets a confidence, 0 to 1;

16
00:00:34,270 --> 00:00:35,988
unsure ones can go to a person.

17
00:00:36,488 --> 00:00:38,350
Up to 1,000 questions per call,

18
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:39,807
the claim sent once.

19
00:00:40,107 --> 00:00:41,430
Google's own benchmarks:

20
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:45,534
nearly 5 times cheaper than Gemini 3.5 Flash-Lite.

21
00:00:46,034 --> 00:00:46,800
So, what we learned:

22
00:00:47,290 --> 00:00:49,890
in our example, 20 questions took 20 prompts;

23
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:51,100
now 1 call,

24
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:52,973
each answer with how sure it is.

25
00:00:53,473 --> 00:00:54,175
Want more?

26
00:00:54,475 --> 00:00:57,006
Subscribe, and comment what we should explain next.
